DTEND:20260730T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260730T000000Z
SUMMARY:History of the White House\, America's Executive Mansion
DESCRIPTION:With Patricia Wells\n\nConstruction took eight years\; a fire 
 nearly destroyed it\, it came dangerously close to collapsing\, and someone 
 almost painted it black. Although President Washington chose the design\, 
 he never really lived there. However\, although Jefferson\, Taft\, and the 
 Roosevelts made exceptional modifications\, one of their genius architects 
 came dangerously close to destroying the structure. It is the edifice that 
 has been the most easily recognizable across the country for the past 225 
 years.\n\nAbout the presenter\n\nPatricia Wells\, a historian and 
 storyteller\, regularly presents all around the state of New Jersey as part 
 of her program called History Is Speaking. These lectures cover various 
 local history topics\, including the American Revolution\, the Great 
 Swamp\, and courageous historical women. The public libraries and 
 historical societies that host her presentations\, both in person and 
 virtually\, are frequent hosts of her events.\n\nThis event will be held in 
 the Community Room 1
LOCATION:West Orange - Community Room 1
ORGANIZER;CN="West Orange Library":MAILTO:events@westorange.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Lecture
UID:LibCal-10341-16960499
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16960499
END:VEVENT
